14. INTELLIGENT PRE-ORDERING AND KITCHEN SCHEDULING ENGINE

This is the core logic of KANTRO AI.

Do not treat pre-ordering as an ordinary add-to-cart feature. The scheduling engine must be connected to the backend.

When a student places an order:

1. Read the selected menu items and quantities.
2. Read the preparation duration of each item.
3. Identify the required kitchen station for each item.
4. Check ingredient availability.
5. Read the student's break start and end times.
6. Read the requested pickup time.
7. Check the station workload during the required preparation interval.
8. Calculate whether the order can be completed before the requested pickup time.
9. If capacity is available, reserve the required capacity.
10. Confirm the order.
11. Send the order to the kitchen employee dashboard.

The backend must prevent two students from reserving the same remaining capacity simultaneously.

Use database transactions or equivalent atomic reservation logic.

If a slot becomes full between checking availability and confirming the order, reject that reservation and ask the student to choose another slot.

Preparation time must be calculated based on the selected items and quantities.

When multiple items use the same kitchen station, account for the combined workload rather than simply displaying the preparation time of one item.

The system should support configurable preparation intervals and kitchen station capacities.

Do not allow a student to select an impossible pickup time.

